        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 1004,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 672,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Answers True only if EVERY value in an array is true (non-zero), and False as soon as a single one is zero.\n\nUse it to check that a condition holds everywhere: are all joints inside their range, did every sensor report, is every value finite, has every step finished. It turns a whole mask into one yes or no.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.all\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.all\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a single number counts as a one-element array). Every array that arrives is tested and the answer sent - this is the only inlet. Any shape is accepted, and the WHOLE array is always tested: there is no axis, so you get one True or False, never one per row. Values are read as true or false the numpy way: 0, 0.0 and False are false, anything else - including negative numbers and NaN - is true.\nAn empty array gives True (there is nothing false in it).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nTrue or False.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.rand makes 6 by 6 values between 0.2 and 0.8. The > node compares each with the slider and draws the answer as a mask: white where the value is above the threshold, dark red where it is not. With the slider at 0 every cell is white and np.all lights the toggle. Drag the slider up: as soon as the FIRST cell goes dark - the smallest value, just over 0.2 - np.all turns False, even though nearly all the mask is still white. Click np.rand's button for new values.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.any is the opposite question: true if ANY value is true. np.sum of a mask counts how many are true; np.all is the same as that count equalling the number of cells. np.amin > threshold is the same test as np.all of (array > threshold). t.all is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 656,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Answers True only if EVERY value in an array is true (non-zero), and False as soon as a single one is zero.\n\nUse it to check that a condition holds everywhere: are all joints inside their range, did every sensor report, is every value finite, has every step finished. It turns a whole mask into one yes or no.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.all\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.all\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one; a single number counts as a one-element array). Every array that arrives is tested and the answer sent - this is the only inlet. Any shape is accepted, and the WHOLE array is always tested: there is no axis, so you get one True or False, never one per row. Values are read as true or false the numpy way: 0, 0.0 and False are false, anything else - including negative numbers and NaN - is true.\nAn empty array gives True (there is nothing false in it).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nTrue or False.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.rand makes 6 by 6 values between 0.2 and 0.8. The > node compares each with the slider and draws the answer as a mask: white where the value is above the threshold, dark red where it is not. With the slider at 0 every cell is white and np.all lights the toggle. Drag the slider up: as soon as the FIRST cell goes dark - the smallest value, just over 0.2 - np.all turns False, even though nearly all the mask is still white. Click np.rand's button for new values.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.any is the opposite question: true if ANY value is true. np.sum of a mask counts how many are true; np.all is the same as that count equalling the number of cells. np.amin > threshold is the same test as np.all of (array > threshold). t.all is the PyTorch version."
        },
